// Releasing a long chain does not recurse on the C stack.
//
// It used to: an object's fields are released from inside its drop
// function, so freeing a linked list cost one C frame per link. 100 000
// nodes segfaulted at -O0 and 200 000 at -O2, on the default 8 MiB stack
// (docs/destructors-decision.md). The depth is the data's, and the program
// chooses the data, so no stack size was the answer -- `rc_dec` now keeps
// the pending work on the heap and runs one frame deep whatever the shape.
//
// 200 000 nodes is two objects a link, the `Node` and the `Option` that
// holds it: both counts above crashed before, and the number is kept small
// enough that the corpus runs it four times under two sanitizers without
// taking noticeably longer.
type Node {
    int v;
    Option<Node> next;
}

Option<Node> chain = Option<Node>.None;
int i = 0;
while (i < 200000) {
    chain = Option<Node>.Some(Node(i, chain));
    i = i + 1;
}
print(i);
// `chain` is released when the program body ends -- 400 000 objects, one
// after another, and the refcount invariant at the bottom of the run proves
// every one of them went.
